\p{Filed under [[conf-neuromod-nyc-2026]] as a methods/modeling entry (it sits under all three device lenses rather than in one). No people in the photo.}

\image{conf-neuromodec-hh-operators.jpg}

\p{\strong{Caption.} Fourier and Laplace \strong{Neural Operators} (FNO / LNO) trained to reproduce stiff \strong{Hodgkin-Huxley} membrane dynamics orders of magnitude faster than conventional ODE solvers, evaluated by a rate-of-agreement metric across DC/AC/AC+DC stimulus regimes. The fast forward-model layer under any simulation-kernel voting scheme like ours ([[bcf-devices]]): if HH runs in real time, per-vote world-simulation becomes cheap.}

\subtree{\title{Conversation note}
\taxon{remark}
\p{From talking with the author: the model is currently limited to \strong{single-membrane potentiation} modeling, which is compute-constrained, and he agreed it is only really meaningful for \strong{large-scale brain behavior once embedded in a bigger network} — a single membrane in isolation does not tell you much. This is \strong{consistent with the other Hodgkin-Huxley discussion} at the conference: the value is in the network, and the point of making the single-unit solve cheap (via neural operators) is precisely to afford the network scale.}
}
